'use strict';
/* ============================================================================
   playermodel.js — the astronaut, baked from a skinned glTF.

   Unlike the starship, this model really is skinned: every vertex is weighted
   to up to four joints and has to be deformed on the GPU.  The source rig had
   102 joints, but over half of them were twist, share and finger bones that
   exist to fix deformation detail a third-person character never shows.  They
   are pruned here and their skin weights folded into the nearest surviving
   ancestor, which leaves 25 joints — few enough to pass the skinning palette as
   plain uniforms instead of a bone texture.

   The mesh arrived as 299988 triangles across six body parts, each with its own
   1024 texture.  Each part is decimated with quadric edge collapses and its
   UVs, normals and skin weights carried across by nearest-vertex transfer; the
   five textures become one atlas.  41995 triangles survive.

   The source carries exactly one animation: a 10.6 s idle.  There is no walk,
   no run and no jump in it.  Those are generated at runtime in player.js by
   rotating a handful of named joints on top of this idle — see the locomotion
   section there.  That is why the joint names are kept in the file.
   ============================================================================ */

